  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000005: random_greedy_2opt\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 32.8364\n\nThought: Construct initial tour using randomized greedy that selects from nearest 3 unvisited cities. Then apply 2-opt improvement until convergence or budget exhausted.\n\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: tsp:size:500\nHistory role: regime specialist\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport random\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, seed: int, budget: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    random.seed(seed)\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    if n <= 2:\n        tour = np.arange(n, dtype=np.int32)\n        return tour\n    # Randomized greedy construction\n    unvisited = set(range(n))\n    start = random.randrange(n)\n    tour = [start]\n    unvisited.remove(start)\n    while unvisited:\n        current = tour[-1]\n        # Get distances to unvisited\n        cand = list(unvisited)\n        dists = [(distance_matrix[current, c], c) for c in cand]\n        dists.sort()\n        # Choose among the 3 nearest (or less if fewer)\n        k = min(3, len(dists))\n        choice = random.randrange(k)\n        next_city = dists[choice][1]\n        tour.append(next_city)\n        unvisited.remove(next_city)\n    tour = np.array(tour, dtype=np.int32)\n    best_tour = tour.copy()\n    best_dist = sum(distance_matrix[tour[i], tour[(i+1)%n]] for i in range(n))\n    # 2-opt improvement\n    iteration = 0\n    improved = True\n    while improved and iteration < budget:\n        improved = False\n        for i in range(n-2):\n            for j in range(i+2, n):\n                iteration += 1\n                if iteration >= budget:\n                    break\n                # Check if reversing segment (i+1..j) reduces distance\n                a, b, c, d = tour[i], tour[i+1], tour[j], tour[(j+1)%n]\n                delta = distance_matrix[a, c] + distance_matrix[b, d] - distance_matrix[a, b] - distance_matrix[c, d]\n                if delta < -1e-12:\n                    # Apply 2-opt move\n                    tour[i+1:j+1] = tour[i+1:j+1][::-1]\n                    best_tour = tour.copy()\n                    # Update best_dist (not necessary but for potential use)\n                    best_dist = best_dist + delta\n                    improved = True\n                    report_best_tour(best_tour)\n                    break\n            if improved or iteration >= budget:\n                break\n    return best_tour\n\n```\n\nSTRATEGY: m4_contract_repair\n\nGOAL: Revise one parent to better satisfy the required interface, constraints, safety rules, and budget limits. Preserve the main algorithmic idea, but fix invalid return paths, unsafe assumptions, missing fallback behavior, and fragile edge cases.\n\nUse the provided parent(s) as the primary source of design changes.\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement this function only:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, seed: int, budget: int) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour, even when budget is very small.\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates.\n- Use seed for randomness.\n- Treat budget as a hard cap on search effort.\n- Create a valid tour early.\n- Call report_best_tour(tour) whenever a new best valid tour is found, assume this func already exists.\n- Do not use files, network, subprocesses, or external solvers.\n- Keep the code compact and robust.\n\ncorrect signature, valid tour on every return path,\nearly report_best_tour, budget-bounded main search, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"nam